# Kotlin Gray-Part — Keyboard

The requirement is one line — the focused field must not end up under the
keyboard — and it took more attempts than anything else in the flow. Read this
before writing a single line of keyboard code; every simpler approach listed
under "what does not work" was tried on a real device and rejected by the
client, in that order.

---

## What does not work

**`adjustResize` and let the page cope.** Taking height from a WebView shrinks
the page's visual viewport, and the engine answers with a burst of work in one
go: it re-lays out, scrolls the focused field into view against the layout it
had a moment earlier, then clamps that scroll to the layout that finally lands.
The last two steps disagree, and their disagreement is the visible flick.

**`adjustResize` plus a JS `scrollIntoView` on `focusin`.** Now two parties
scroll the same page. The engine's own correction and the injected one add up,
the content shoots past the field and settles back a frame or two later. Using
`block:'center'`, `behavior:'auto'`, or a delay only changes how far it
overshoots.

**Animated IME padding on the container.** Same doubling, with easing on top of
it. This one was reported as *worse* than doing nothing.

**Panning natively while the engine still sees the keyboard.** The engine's
own shift is not a constant: on one device, on the very same field, it moved
the page 132 px on one opening and 737 px on the next. Anything added on top
inherits that randomness.

Everything above shares one cause: **two things moving the page at once.** The
fix is not better easing, it is making sure only one of them moves it.

---

## What works: pan the view, and blind the engine

1. **Never resize.** The WebView keeps its full height; it is slid with
   `translationY`. The page never reflows, and the move costs one compositor
   frame. The strip left bare at the bottom is always shorter than the
   keyboard, so the keyboard covers it.
2. **Keep the IME insets away from the WebView.** A
   `WindowInsetsAnimationCompat.Callback` with `DISPATCH_MODE_STOP` on the
   window root, plus an insets listener that rebuilds the insets with
   `Type.ime()` set to `Insets.NONE` and invisible. Now the engine has nothing
   to react to and the native pan is the only hand on the wheel.
3. **Ask the page where the field is,** in device pixels, over a
   `@JavascriptInterface`. Not as a fraction of the viewport — that viewport is
   exactly the thing that can change under the measurement.
4. **Ride the keyboard up.** `onProgress` gives the IME height every frame;
   the pan is recomputed and assigned directly (no animator) each time, so it
   moves in step with the keyboard rather than chasing it.
5. **Clamp to the height the keyboard will actually settle at** — see below,
   this is the part that is easy to miss and produces the last remaining
   "content flies up and comes back".

The container itself must then apply **cutout padding only** and leave bottom
padding at zero. Any IME padding left in the container fights the pan.

---

## The settled-height trap

Mid-animation the system reports IME heights well above the one it settles on.
Measured on a Mi 9T: **913 against a final 833** upright, **771 against 683** on
its side. A pan that follows those to the letter sails past its mark and drops
back at the very end — a small, sharp jerk, and the last one to survive every
other fix.

`bounds.upperBound` from `onStart` is not the answer either: it reported the
correct 833 on the first opening of an orientation and a useless 913 on every
later one.

The only figure that never lied is a height an earlier opening actually came to
rest at. So:

- remember it **per orientation**, and **persist it** (SharedPreferences), so
  even the first opening of a session is exact;
- clamp every mid-animation height to it;
- fall back to `upperBound` only when nothing is on record yet — which, with
  persistence, is once per orientation per install;
- reset the in-memory copy on rotation and re-read the stored value for the new
  orientation;
- ignore a stored height that is not smaller than the window (it belongs to
  another device or another window).

---

## Aiming

- **Ordinary field:** aim at its top plus ~96 dp, not its bottom. Hauling a
  long box up by its foot takes its head, and the caret with it, off screen.
- **Rich editor (`contentEditable`):** measure the caret rect from the
  selection, not the editor box.
- **Cross-origin iframe** (sign-in forms often live in one): the frame's own
  rectangle is all there is. Aim at its bottom, but never far enough to push
  its top off screen — the field could be up there.
- Add ~10 CSS px of breathing room under the target.
- Re-read the field position ~200 ms after focus (the page may move it) and
  ~140 ms after the animation ends. Both readings feed the same absolute
  target, so a later one corrects an earlier one instead of adding to it.
- Clear the stored position on every `onPageStarted` and on rotation.

